{"authored" [{"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "07b2b7d3ceffd11231d9ab8bf19b1a2456c07f7e5b6581526db67868862b9772" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "45c5e175cafa8665f153bf96d8285242e4662cf29b0c58ce20ac7fe572dc485b" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "45c5e175cafa8665f153bf96d8285242e4662cf29b0c58ce20ac7fe572dc485b" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "955df653b9e0475549b7b74de471662e7b66593e9c8626032c24c93fd024d614" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "955df653b9e0475549b7b74de471662e7b66593e9c8626032c24c93fd024d614" "basis" 1555 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-113769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}$ on the closed interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-113769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2(x+1){e}^{x}-(x+1)^{2}({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+2-{x}^{2}-2x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{(-1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1}}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=\\frac{4}{e},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{4}{e}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-1)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/e21d39f09f56fa1113184ce7a778d98561b82b932cd324ee90cf3aaff4d729d9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-113769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-113769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-113769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4e"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{e}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4e"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{e}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-113769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\n- The global minimum value is $e-2$\n- The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ on the closed interval $[3,5].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2)({x}^{2}-1)-(2x)(2x)}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-2-4{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(1+{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x,$ with $x\\ne \\pm 1.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=3,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =\\frac{2(3)}{(3)^{2}-1}=\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{2(5)}{(5)^{2}-1}=\\frac{10}{24}=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(3)=\\frac{3}{4},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(5)=\\frac{5}{12},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{5}{12}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/14747ab1b46e589f0692d9e3dc5ffbe91eb1d60e3641d47d1a5eacb5f1df1130.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-20952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $4$\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-6x \\\\  & =2x(2{x}^{2}-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2x(2{x}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$ do not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-3(-1)^{2}+4=2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-3(0)^{2}+4=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=f(1)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/a7141a342d971d5b520b2f7f07f8fdb1c01ddca18a983fb0c83102a5bd4aee8e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-2691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $x=\\pi ,$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1-(-1)=2 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6b8ac504d93007049795ee92eb8865225751314478d68ace67878e27ed471b24.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-344477" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344477" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 2$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}} & =1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x+4} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{15}{4}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2],$ the critical points are the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\sqrt{-1+4}-(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+4}-2=\\sqrt{6}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1,$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{3}+1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=\\sqrt{6}-2,$ so the global minimum is $\\sqrt{6}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/ade599dd27bc3d018a3604620f3741ae05f8ebcc8ff583994da155ce7f311146.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-344464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-344464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0807b4b4822f30f8557b9e3aa1d292b6b21d0d3f478e4332c245def94cb60954"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "q-22615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/state.json" "file_sha256" "c40b53d6e8c0354eb287c2a77b63a78f9cb9d22f66d51806beb165a1465c0fc3"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "77eca384af9098a4d1d5bcaa0a32910f45c8120e4015a21035e177a869603adf"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "77eca384af9098a4d1d5bcaa0a32910f45c8120e4015a21035e177a869603adf"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "3d1431991a592aa0514bc253ff3633956fc78f032d6e1514cee971d65466efa1"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "3d1431991a592aa0514bc253ff3633956fc78f032d6e1514cee971d65466efa1"}] "usage" {"q-22428" [] "e-3737" [] "e-3736" [] "q-314729" [] "q-79342" [] "q-79348" []} "basis" 2749 "retractions" [[17592186064528 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor the function $f(x) = x - 2\\ln x$, defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2} \\le x \\le e$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2} + 2\\ln 2$\n2. The global minimum value is $e - 2$\n3. The global minimum value is $2 - 2\\ln 2$"] [17592186316031 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)"] [17592186316038 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)"] [17592186316022 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)"] [17592186063962 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)"] [17592186063963 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186061700 17592186061701 17592186061702 17592186061703 17592186061704 17592186316024 17592186316033 17592186316034 17592186316035 17592186316036 17592186316037 17592186316040 17592186316041 17592186316042 17592186316043 17592186316044] "immutable_fields" [[17592186061699 :answer-field/correct] [17592186061699 :answer-field/id] [17592186061699 :answer-field/key] [17592186061699 :answer-field/type] [17592186316023 :answer-field/correct] [17592186316023 :answer-field/id] [17592186316023 :answer-field/key] [17592186316023 :answer-field/type] [17592186316032 :answer-field/correct] [17592186316032 :answer-field/id] [17592186316032 :answer-field/key] [17592186316032 :answer-field/type] [17592186316039 :answer-field/correct] [17592186316039 :answer-field/id] [17592186316039 :answer-field/key] [17592186316039 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
